{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible without smuggling in induction if reinterpreted as a non-epistemic, methodological rule of 'critical selection' rather than an expectation of future success; however, accepting this defense costs the scientist any rational assurance that the chosen theory will work in practice, reducing scientific action to a blind evolutionary gamble.","counterconditions":["If the Tichý/Miller proof holds that verisimilitude cannot be estimated, then the 'better' in 'better-corroborated' loses its normative force for truth-likeness.","If acting on a theory (e.g., building a bridge) inherently requires an assumption of its reliability, then merely labeling the preference 'methodological' is a semantic evasion of the pragmatic need for induction.","If Popper's own advice to 'act on the best tested theory' implies an expectation of success, then his practice contradicts his strict prohibition against inductive inference."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The mechanism distinguishes between 'corroboration' (a logical report of past performance) and 'prediction' (an inductive leap). Popper's preference is framed not as believing Theory A is more likely true than Theory B, but as a procedural commitment to test the most vulnerable (high content) theory that has so far survived. The preference is a rule of the game of science, akin to a chess player preferring a gambit that has worked before, not because it guarantees a win, but because it offers the fastest route to checkmate or refutation. This avoids induction by refusing to assign higher probability to the future success of the gambit.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85}],"simulation_proposals":[]}